EXT. MANGROVE SWAMP - WRECKED FERRY - DUSK

A half-sunk river ferry heeled over in brown water, its upper deck tilting among the roots. Mosquitoes hang in the air like smoke. Egrets lift from the branches as the light dims.

On the high side of the deck, three survivors huddle around a cooler. MIRIAM OSEI-STEELE, forties, a nurse, kneels with a gash on her forearm bound in a torn shirt. DESMOND "DEZ" LACROIX, fifties, a ferry mate, grips a boat hook. And a young backpacker, FREYA NIEMI, twenties, sits with her knees up, a camera strap around her wrist.

Miriam lifts the cooler lid and counts the remaining water bottles, and Dez watches the tree line while Freya bites the skin around her thumb. In the near channel, something large slides beneath the surface and the three of them go still as one, then look at each other, then at the water.

MIRIAM
(to the group)
Nobody goes near the rail tonight.

DEZ
(low, to Miriam)
She's bleeding through.

FREYA
(to herself)
It's fine. It's fine.

MIRIAM
Freya, I need you to look at me. Not the water.

Freya lifts her eyes. Miriam peels back the shirt on her own arm, probes the cut, and winces.

MIRIAM (CONT'D)
(muttering)
Infected by morning if we don't clean it.

DEZ
We have the vodka.

MIRIAM
You are not wasting the vodka.

DEZ
It's for antiseptic.

MIRIAM
It's for emergencies.

DEZ
This is an emergency.

MIRIAM
This is a Tuesday.

Dez laughs, startling himself. Freya laughs, and then stops, because she is crying. Miriam shifts to sit beside her, and Dez, awkward with the boat hook, drops it and puts a rough arm around Freya's shoulders from the other side. The three of them rock in the dying light.

FREYA
(whispering)
I wanted to see the herons.

DEZ
You saw the herons.

FREYA
I saw them leave.

Above them, the first star appears over the canopy. The cooler lid clatters shut in a breeze. Miriam holds out the vodka bottle to Dez without looking at him, and he takes it. He pours a little over her arm, and she hisses through her teeth, and he pours a little for himself.
